Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that provisions and manages infrastructure by describing what you want, not by scripting every step. In Terraform, you write a configuration that declares desired resources such as networks, instances, buckets, and databases, and Terraform works out how to reach that end state.

Most Terraform configurations are written in HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL), which focuses on declarations rather than step-by-step commands. A key building block is the resource, which represents an infrastructure object you want Terraform to manage. Terraform also uses providers, which translate Terraform resource operations into platform or service API calls, so the same Terraform workflow can manage different platforms as long as the appropriate provider is available.

Terraform’s dependency graph is what makes the workflow reliable. When one resource references another, Terraform records that relationship and uses it to determine the correct operation order. For example, if a compute instance depends on a network, Terraform can ensure the network is created before the instance, and it can also order updates and deletions safely.

Terraform supports reusable structure and clearer configuration through variables, locals, outputs, expressions, and modules. Variables parameterize configurations so you can reuse the same pattern with different values. Locals simplify repeated expressions, outputs expose useful values from a deployment, and modules package related resources so teams can share and reuse infrastructure building blocks.

Here is a minimal HCL example that shows a required provider, creates one real resource, and defines an output.

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "example" {
  bucket = "my-unique-bucket-name-12345"
}

output "bucket_name" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.example.bucket
}
```

In this example, the output depends on the resource attribute, which is a concrete reference that helps Terraform build the dependency graph and plan changes correctly.
